WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.360
Speaker 1: Why don't we keep it going?

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:07.310
It's hard to imagine we could keep such a
streak going today, but I'm really going to

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.660
try.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:15.670
Let's go to and I know that last time I mispronounced
your name, is it to Meesha from New York City.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.890
Yes, Tamasha, how are you?

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.000
Speaker 3: Hi, how are you doing?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.450
David, do you know why I like you so much?

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.770
Speaker 1: No, I'm shocked you do, to be honest.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:32.550
Speaker 3: No, I like you because I think
of things and I see things happening, and

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.820
then I go to watch a video of yours and you
think exactly are similar to the way I think,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.820
OK.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.190
But I wanted to ask you a question about.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:51.140
I know that there's been a policy in the talks
about free college.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.980
I know Biden was talking about community colleges
being free.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.160
Yeah, I know some people talk about state
colleges.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.880
And when I talk to my friends about it, I
say, oh, my son was 11.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.880
By the time he goes to college, it'll be free.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.170
And when we talk about college funds.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.810
But then they told me that college is only
free for, like, the really, really poor.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.240
Is that true, that it won't be like everybody
has access to that?

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.610
Speaker 1: Well, the thing the thing that's
difficult to measure is that we don't know

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:25.960
yet because there are proposals, there are
proposals for full free state college regardless

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.340
of income.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:33.950
There are proposals for means tested programs
where if family income is above a certain

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.340
amount, then it's not free, but below that
amount it is free.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.300
But what I will tell you is this.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:48.360
Every proposal I've seen from any any serious
Democrat would be at least free community

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.159
college below very.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.650
It won't only be for people under the federal
poverty line.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.600
It won't be that it'll be free for a significant
portion of the population.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.030
The real question is, will this pass?

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.030
I have no idea.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.030
We just don't know yet.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.820
For now, there's you know, nothing has passed.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:09.130
So if it were me right now and I don't have
a kid right now to save for college, I would

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:13.810
be operating financially under the assumption
that college would not be free for them.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:14.810
And, hey, you know what?

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.560
If it ends up being free, that's great.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.959
Speaker 3: Well, yeah, I guess I'm being optimistic.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:24.370
Yeah, of course, I do agree with you that
you should plan for any outcome.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:30.000
Yes, but and I think I find, like, the fact
that it wouldn't be like the income part of

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.530
it bothers me only a little bit because, you
know, most people, even if you have a decent

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.870
salary, you still fall and you're still not
necessarily rich.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.530
Of course, when you know, even if you have
a decent salary and those people still tend

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.530
to be forgotten.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.300
Speaker 1: No doubt, and this was an issue
sometimes, you know, with with Obamacare in

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.650
many states, the way that the subsidies are
set, there's no I don't remember what state

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.370
it was, but there are states where the subsidies,
if you're a self-employed person and you have

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:03.650
to go and buy a plan on the health care market,
you get a really big subsidy.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.071
If you earn under ten thousand dollars a year
and a smaller subsidy between 10 and 20 and

00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:09.819
a smaller subsidy between 20 and 30.

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:12.981
But like by the time you get to forty eight
thousand dollars a year, the subsidy ends.

00:03:12.981 --> 00:03:17.690
So basically, if you earn forty eight thousand
dollars a year, eighty eight thousand dollars

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.880
a year or eight hundred and eighty eight thousand
dollars a year, everybody gets no subsidy.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.250
Once you get to forty eight, that seems terrible.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.650
It's that middle that really gets squeezed
in that situation.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.849
And so something to be aware of.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:34.920
And let's hope that it doesn't happen if indeed
there's a college funding plan put put together.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.010
Speaker 3: Yes, hopefully.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.590
One more thing.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:44.030
I know you talked about this a week or so
ago, two weeks maybe about the mayor in Chicago.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.459
I think it was what she was saying, that she
would only

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:51.210
Speaker 1: she would only be interviewed by
nonwhite journalists.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.590
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:58.709
And I agree with you where I feel like if
you discriminate, if you are calling out discrimination

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:03.310
by using discrimination, and that's not only
hypocritical, but it defeats the purpose.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:04.310
Yes.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:09.401
But also from another perspective, I think
I'm I'm guessing I can't speak for her, but

00:04:09.401 --> 00:04:14.260
I'm thinking that since, you know, the history
of blacks in this country is where we're seen

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:24.059
as less than we're seen as not human in the
past or were seen as a stupid, lazy like those

00:04:24.059 --> 00:04:26.369
are some of the stereotypes.

00:04:26.369 --> 00:04:31.419
I think that that what she was trying to do
or what maybe she's trying to do is say we're

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:34.479
trying to I'm trying to even the playing field
and give us an upper hand.

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:36.419
We're always
Speaker 1: upper hand.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:37.419
Yeah.

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:40.939
And to clarify on that, to me, for people
who don't know, Chicago Mayor Lori Lightfoot

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:42.090
said that around.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.309
I don't remember if it was just like around
Memorial Day or what it was.

00:04:45.309 --> 00:04:51.460
She said that she was only going to be interviewed
by nonwhite journalists for this short period

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.169
of time and interview requests by white journalists
would be rejected.

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.949
Now, what I said at the time was, if this
is just a policy, this is this is just racism

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:01.800
and I'm against it completely.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.020
However, if it had been presented as, hey,
you know what, this is a city, the city of

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.779
Chicago, which has a huge nonwhite population.

00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:15.259
And yet most of the interviews with elected
officials that show up in the newspapers are

00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:16.259
with white journalists.

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:23.189
So for this one week, I am going to prioritize
being interviewed by journalists of color.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:25.249
Great, beautiful.

00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:30.869
However, the way that it was said was I will
be rejecting interview requests from white

00:05:30.869 --> 00:05:31.869
journalists.

00:05:31.869 --> 00:05:33.050
And you know what?

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:35.919
I just think it was it was completely, improperly
framed.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:38.499
And I agree with you that there's a right
way to do this and there's a wrong way to

00:05:38.499 --> 00:05:39.499
do this.

00:05:39.499 --> 00:05:42.349
Speaker 3: Yeah, and that's basically what
is trying to figure out the right way.

00:05:42.349 --> 00:05:43.349
Yeah.

00:05:43.349 --> 00:05:47.339
So give us an even playing field, because
most of the time what when black people are

00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:50.080
asking for is equality not necessarily a handout.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.080
Right.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.080
We take it.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.589
It's just to even the playing field.

00:05:53.589 --> 00:05:54.719
I'm with you.

00:05:54.719 --> 00:05:56.559
That's not always the right way to do it.

00:05:56.559 --> 00:05:57.559
I agree with you.

00:05:57.559 --> 00:06:00.779
Speaker 1: All right, Tamika, always great
to hear from you.

00:06:00.779 --> 00:06:01.779
Thank you.

00:06:01.779 --> 00:06:02.779
All right.

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:03.779
There is to Meesha.

